==Post-show career==

After winning the competition, [[Edd Kimber]] is able to make his living from baking.<ref name="metro">{{cite web |url=http://metro.co.uk/2012/08/28/edd-kimber-and-jo-wheatley-winning-the-great-british-bake-off-was-life-changing-554519/ |title= Edd Kimber and Jo Wheatley: Great British Bake Off changed our lives |date=28 August 2012 |work=Metro }}</ref> He worked in the pastry kitchen at [[Raymond Blanc]]'s restaurant Le Manoir.  He has written three books on baking, ''The Boy Who Bakes'', ''Say It With Cake'',<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/tv/features/great-british-bake-off-winners-where-are-they-now-8897298.html?action=gallery |title=Great British Bake Off winners: Where are they now? |author=Daisy Wyatt |date =22 October 2013 |work=The Independent}}</ref> and ''Patisserie Made Simple: From Macaron to Millefeuille and More''. He has taught macaron making classes in London, run a pop-up bakery in [[Fortnum & Mason]], and appeared as a 'resident baker' on ''[[The Alan Titchmarsh Show]]''.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/tv/news/great-british-bake-off-2014-what-past-winners-are-doing-now-from-edd-kimber-to-last-years-frances-quinn-9780390.html |archive-url=https://ghostarchive.org/archive/20220618/https://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/tv/news/great-british-bake-off-2014-what-past-winners-are-doing-now-from-edd-kimber-to-last-years-frances-quinn-9780390.html |archive-date=18 June 2022 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |title=Great British Bake Off 2014: What past winners are doing now from Edd Kimber to last year's Frances Quinn |author=Jess Denham |date=7 October 2014 |work=The Independent }}</ref>

Ruth Clemens has written a number of books on cake making and baking, including ''Busy Girls Guide to Cake Decorating'', ''The Pink Whisk Guide to Cake Making'', ''The Pink Whisk Guide to Baking'' and ''Creative Éclairs''.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.mumsintheknow.co.uk/poynton/2012/12/02/poynton-mum-in-the-spotlight-ruth-clemens-the-pink-whisk/ |title=Poynton Mum in the Spotlight – Ruth Clemens – The Pink Whisk! |work=Mums in the Know |date=2 December 2012 |url-status=dead |archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20150412115629/http://www.mumsintheknow.co.uk/poynton/2012/12/02/poynton-mum-in-the-spotlight-ruth-clemens-the-pink-whisk/ |archivedate=12 April 2015 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.thediaryofacakemaker.com/interview-ruth-clemens-pink-whisk/ |title=Interview with Ruth Clemens, The Pink Whisk |date=7 March 2014 |work=The Diary of a Cake Maker }}</ref>

Miranda Gore Browne wrote a book, ''Biscuit'', published on 5 April 2012.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.peachtreesandbumblebees.com/2013/06/13/interview-miranda-gore-browne/ |title=Interview & cookbook giveaway: Miranda Gore Browne, Great British Bake Off |date =13 June 2013 |work=Peach Trees and Bumblebees }}</ref> Her second book, ''Bake Me a Cake as Fast as You Can'', was published in August 2014.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/foodanddrink/11087337/Cookbook-of-the-Week-Bake-Me-A-Cake-As-Fast-As-You-Can.html |title=Cookbook of the Week: Bake Me A Cake As Fast As You Can |author=Carolyn Hart|date= 11 September 2014 |work=The Daily Telegraph }}</ref>

Jonathan Shepherd left his job in the pharmaceutical company and launched a new business, The Pudding Stop, in St Albans.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.hertsad.co.uk/news/st_albans_baker_takes_the_plunge_with_puddings_1_633847 |title=St Albans baker takes the plunge with puddings |date=9 September 2010 |author=Charlotte Morgan |work=The Herts Advertiser }}</ref>  It started as a portable street-food vendor and a supplier of puddings to restaurants.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.hertsad.co.uk/news/st_albans_pudding_stop_shortlisted_for_award_1_1583674|title=St Albans' Pudding Stop shortlisted for award |date=8 October 2012 |author=Monique Hall |work=The Herts Advertiser }}</ref> Shepherd has since also opened a shop, also in St Albans.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.lovefood.com/guide/chefs/17807/great-british-bake-off-winners-where-are-they-now |title=Great British Bake Off: where are they now?|author= Charlotte Morgan  |date=  19 August 2014 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.thepuddingstop.com/ |title=The Pudding Stop }}</ref>

Mark Whithers, who was eliminated in Episode 1, died on 11 May 2013 after suffering from cancer.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Hope|first=Hannah|last2=Rowney|first2=Jo-Anne|last3=Hards|first3=Shannon|date=2018-09-11|title=Where are the past GBBO winners now?|url=https://www.cornwalllive.com/news/celebs-tv/past-great-british-bake-winners-1993125|access-date=2020-11-04|website=CornwallLive|language=en}}</ref>

Lea Harris participated in the 2024 rendition of ''The Great New Year's Bake Off''.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.digitalspy.com/tv/reality-tv/a62916394/bake-off-2024-christmas-new-year-cast/ |title=Bake Off confirms soaps special with Christmas and New Year lineups|date=15 November 2024|work=Digital Spy|accessdate=18 November 2025}}</ref>

==The Great British Wedding Cake==
In this one-off special edition, the three finalists from series one (Edd, Ruth and Miranda) returned. [[Paul Hollywood]] and [[Mary Berry (food writer)|Mary Berry]] set them the challenge of making and baking two spectacular [[Wedding Cake]]s each in just sixteen hours, one being ''traditional'' and the other ''contemporary''.
Paul and Mary explore the history of the Great British Wedding Cake, looking at the dramatic changes through the eras; from the Tudors and the Victorians, wartime Britain and the eighties to the present day.
Although Paul and Mary judged the results, no individual winner was declared.

